    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#basil-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basil-ii.jpg" alt="Basil II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#basil-ii">Basil II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilios_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">from the Middle Ages, unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For more than a century and a half after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basil-ii">Basil II</a> completed the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> in 1018, the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a> were Byzantine provinces. Discontent grew under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#angelos-dynasty">Angelos</a> emperors, and in 1185 an extraordinary tax levied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a>, linked by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niketas-choniates">Niketas Choniates</a> to the costs of the emperor's wedding to a Hungarian princess, provoked a revolt. Its leaders were two brothers from the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, Theodore, who took the name Peter, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-i">Asen</a>. Their origins, Bulgarian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vlachs">Vlach</a> or Cuman, are still debated, and contemporaries described their followers as both Vlachs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. They proclaimed the rising at the dedication of a church of Saint Demetrius in Tarnovo, presenting the warrior saint as having abandoned Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> for their cause. Driven north of the Danube in 1186, the brothers returned with Cuman allies and repeatedly defeated Byzantine armies, most notably ambushing Isaac II in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-tryavna">Tryavna pass</a> in 1190. Asen was murdered by boyars in 1196 and Peter in 1197, but their younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a> (r. 1197–1207) secured the new state. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#varna">Varna</a>, negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a>, and in 1204 received a royal crown from a papal legate in return for a nominal union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>, winning recognition for the restored monarchy just as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> broke Byzantine power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pomorie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pomorie.jpg" alt="Pomorie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pomorie">Pomorie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Town_of_Pomorie_aerial_Boby_Dimitrov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Boby Dimitrov from Sofia, Bulgaria, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Second Bulgarian Empire was an agrarian society in which most people were dependent peasants working the lands of the crown, the boyars and the monasteries. Its core lay in the fertile valleys of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, but at its height it also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thrace">Thrace</a>, much of Macedonia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast. Grain, livestock, wine, honey, wax and hides were the main products. Ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#varna">Varna</a>, Mesembria (Nesebar) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pomorie">Anchialos</a> linked the country to wider trade, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> granted merchants from Ragusa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>) a charter allowing them to trade freely in his lands; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#venice">Venetians</a> and Genoese became prominent in the fourteenth century, when the tsars issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> coinage in quantity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, built on the fortified hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tsarevets">Tsarevets</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#trapezitsa">Trapezitsa</a>, grew into a city of churches, palaces and monasteries. Orthodoxy was the official faith, but the dualist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bogomilism">Bogomil movement</a> persisted, and in 1211 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#boril-of-bulgaria">Tsar Boril</a> summoned a council at Tarnovo that condemned it. In the fourteenth century Bulgarian monks embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hesychasm">Hesychasm</a>, the contemplative movement associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#theodosius-of-tarnovo">Theodosius of Tarnovo</a> founded the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kilifarevo-monastery">Kilifarevo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#euthymius-of-tarnovo">Euthymius</a>, patriarch from 1375, reformed the spelling and language of church books. Royal and noble patronage produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#boyana-church">Boyana Church</a> frescoes of 1259 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sofia">Sofia</a>, the illuminated Bulgarian translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manasses-chronicle">Manasses Chronicle</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tetraevangelion-of-ivan-alexander">Tetraevangelion of Ivan Alexander</a> of 1355–1356, now in the British Library.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-velbazhd"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-velbazhd.jpg" alt="Battle of Velbazhd" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-velbazhd">Battle of Velbazhd</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Velbu%C5%BEd,_detail_from_Visoki_De%C4%8Dani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">late 16th-century fresco painter of Visoki Dečani, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The armies of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-i">Asen</a> rulers combined Bulgarian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vlachs">Vlach</a> infantry and horsemen with Cuman light cavalry from the steppes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#danube">Danube</a>, a mobile force suited to ambushes in the mountain passes and raids deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thrace">Thrace</a>. Their greatest victory came on 14 April 1205 at the Battle of Adrianople, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a>, allied with Greek nobles of Thrace who resented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> rule, drew the knights of the new Latin Empire into pursuit and destroyed their army; Emperor Baldwin I was captured and died in Bulgarian captivity. Kaloyan himself died in 1207 while besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> (r. 1218–1241) won the Battle of Klokotnitsa on 9 March 1230, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#epirus">Epirus</a> and Thessalonica, and bringing much of Thrace, Macedonia and Albania under Bulgarian rule, as he proclaimed in an inscription in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#church-of-the-forty-martyrs">Church of the Forty Martyrs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>. Later wars went less well. Mongol attacks from 1242 forced the tsars to pay tribute, and in 1277 the swineherd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivailo">Ivailo</a> led a peasant rising that beat off Mongol raiders and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantine-tikh">Constantine Tikh</a>. In 1330 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#serbs">Serbs</a> defeated and killed Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#michael-shishman">Michael Shishman</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-velbazhd">Battle of Velbazhd</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria">Ivan Alexander</a> beat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-rusokastro">Rusokastro</a> in 1332. After their victory over Serbian lords at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-maritsa">Battle of the Maritsa</a> in 1371, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> overran the country, taking Tarnovo in 1393.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii.jpg" alt="Ivan Asen II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Ivan_Asen_II.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The rulers of the Second Bulgarian Empire modelled their state on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> while asserting their independence from it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a> accepted the title of king from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>, but his successors used the title of tsar, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> styled himself tsar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greeks">Greeks</a>. The tsar ruled from the palace on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tsarevets">Tsarevets</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> with a council of great boyars and a court whose dignities, such as despot, sebastokrator, protovestiarios and logothete, were borrowed from Byzantine practice. Provinces were governed by appointed officials or held by members of the royal family and powerful boyars, whose estates and immunities grew over time; by the fourteenth century semi-independent lords controlled regions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dobruja">Dobruja</a>, and the realm was held together more by personal ties than by a salaried bureaucracy. The church was a central pillar of royal authority. Kaloyan's union with Rome lapsed under Ivan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-i">Asen</a> II, who allied with the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nicaea">Nicaea</a>, and in 1235 a council at Lampsacus recognised the archbishop of Tarnovo as patriarch. Royal charters granting land and privileges to monasteries, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-shishman">Ivan Shishman</a>'s charter to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rila-monastery">Rila Monastery</a> of 1378, survive as rare documents of Bulgarian administration. Succession followed no fixed rule, and after the Asen line died out in the 1250s competing dynasties, the Terter and Shishman houses, held the throne, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatar</a>, Serbian and Byzantine interventions repeatedly decided who ruled.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#slavonic-bulgarian-history"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slavonic-bulgarian-history.jpg" alt="Slavonic-Bulgarian History" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#slavonic-bulgarian-history">Slavonic-Bulgarian History</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istoriya-slavyanobulgarska.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">I. Stankov, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Second Bulgarian Empire restored Bulgarian statehood, church and letters after almost two centuries of Byzantine rule, and its legacy reached far beyond its borders. The victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edirne">Adrianople</a> in 1205 helped prevent the Latin Empire from consolidating its hold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balkans">Balkans</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a>'s alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nicaea">Nicaea</a> strengthened the Greek state that recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1261. The restored patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> an autocephalous church of their own. The most lasting impact was cultural. The Tarnovo literary school, associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#euthymius-of-tarnovo">Patriarch Euthymius</a> and his disciples, produced saints' lives, liturgical texts and translations in a carefully revised Church Slavonic. After the Ottoman conquest its scholars carried these standards abroad: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gregory-tsamblak">Gregory Tsamblak</a> worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#serbia">Serbia</a>, Moldavia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyprian">Cyprian</a>, probably a Bulgarian by origin, became metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a>, so that the Tarnovo reforms spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a> in what historians call the second South Slavic influence. In Wallachia and Moldavia, Church Slavonic in its Middle Bulgarian form remained the language of church and chancery for centuries. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> the memory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-i">Asen</a> tsars sustained a sense of nationhood under Ottoman rule; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paisius-of-hilendar">Paisius of Hilendar</a> celebrated them in his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slavonic-bulgarian-history">Slavonic-Bulgarian History</a></em> of 1762, and when a Bulgarian state was restored in 1878, Tarnovo hosted the assembly that adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarnovo-constitution">Tarnovo Constitution</a> of 1879.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-maritsa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa.jpg" alt="Battle of Maritsa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BattleofMaritsa1371.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">South Serbian, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> in 1241 the empire never regained its earlier strength. Mongol invasions from 1242 reduced it to paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nicaea">Nicaea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#epirus">Epirus</a> seized much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-i">Asen</a> line ended in the 1250s amid boyar conspiracies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivailo">Ivailo</a>'s rising of 1277–1280, interference by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatar</a> warlord Nogai and Byzantine intrigue weakened the monarchy further, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#theodore-svetoslav">Theodore Svetoslav</a> (r. 1300–1322) restored some stability and recovered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a> ports. The Serbian victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyustendil">Velbazhd</a> in 1330 confirmed the shift of Balkan leadership to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#serbia">Serbia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria">Ivan Alexander</a> (r. 1331–1371) presided over a cultural flowering, but the realm fragmented: around 1356 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-sratsimir">Ivan Sratsimir</a> established himself as tsar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vidin">Vidin</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dobruja">Dobruja</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balik">Balik</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dobrotitsa">Dobrotitsa</a> went its own way. Meanwhile the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> from 1354, advanced through Thrace. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-maritsa">Battle of the Maritsa</a> in 1371, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-shishman">Ivan Shishman</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#murad-i">Murad I</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> captured Tarnovo on 17 July 1393 after a three-month siege and sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#euthymius-of-tarnovo">Patriarch Euthymius</a> into exile; Ivan Shishman died in 1395, probably executed. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusade">crusade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund of Hungary</a> was crushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a> in 1396, Vidin was annexed, ending the medieval Bulgarian state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> remained part of the Ottoman Empire until 1878.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Second Bulgarian Empire restored Bulgarian independence after nearly two centuries of Byzantine rule, destroyed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> crusader army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edirne">Adrianople</a> in 1205 and briefly dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balkans">Balkans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> literary school it fostered shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Slavic culture from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#serbia">Serbia</a> and Wallachia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a>.</em></p>

